1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,000
A blocked drain can become more than a drainage problem. During heavy rain, it can trigger flooding, road disruption, congestion, and emergency delays.

2
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:20,000
EchoMind helps us explore that chain. This hackathon prototype uses rainfall, drain blockage, and traffic load as scenario inputs.

3
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:28,000
Its scores are transparent, rule-based risk indices—not calibrated probabilities or verified predictions.

4
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:43,000
The map shows illustrative sample locations. Current weather is requested from Open-Meteo when internet access is available. The risk pins are not real flood detections.

5
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:53,000
Users can submit an observation, choose a severity, and add a landmark and coordinates.

6
00:00:53,000 --> 00:01:02,000
Reports are saved locally and appear as purple pins. They remain unverified and are not sent to authorities.

7
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:12,000
The dependency graph connects rainfall and drainage blockage to flooding, road disruption, traffic congestion, emergency delay, and possible power interruption.

8
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:22,000
A simple downstream-impact heuristic suggests which starting hazard deserves attention in this scenario.

9
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:32,000
We can test an intervention such as clearing the critical drain and compare the baseline with the simulated outcome.

10
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:42,000
These changes help structure planning discussions, but they do not prove a real intervention will have the same effect.

11
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:52,000
The next step is validation with trusted local incident data, drainage information, and city partners.

12
00:01:52,000 --> 00:02:00,000
EchoMind — First Domino AI, by BEST OF FOUR. Find the first domino before the impacts spread.
